<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-equilibrium-crossings" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Passing Through Equilibrium</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE + INTERACTIVE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We know the mass oscillates forever in
         free undamped motion. Let's think about a specific question:
         how many times does it pass through equilibrium (x=0) as t goes
         to infinity? Play around with the graph below and see what you notice. 
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-spring-equilibrium">
    <caption>
      Simple harmonic motion <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m>
      with all three parameters adjustable. Each time the mass passes through
      equilibrium (<m>x = 0</m>), a green dot appears on the graph and the
      counter updates. Try different values of <m>c_1</m>, <m>c_2</m>, and
      <m>\omega_0</m>. What do you notice?
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-spring-equilibrium"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-spring-equilibrium.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:3">
      <description>
        An animated spring and position graph. A counter labeled "Times through
        equilibrium (x equals 0)" increments each time the mass crosses the
        equilibrium position. Green dots appear on the graph at each crossing.
        The equilibrium line on the spring flashes green at each crossing.
        Sliders control c1, c2, and omega naught.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Notice that no matter what you set the sliders to (as long as
         the solution isn't identically zero), the mass oscillating with a fixed amplitude, 
         So it keeps crossing the equilibrium (<m>x=0</m>) forever.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-equil-crossings">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Based on the graph above, how many times does a mass undergoing
        free undamped simple harmonic motion pass through equilibrium
        (<m>x = 0</m>) as <m>t \to \infty</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>A finite number of times, depending on <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Try changing <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> in the interactive above --
            the mass keeps crossing equilibrium regardless. The amplitude changes,
            but the oscillation never stops.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>A finite number of times, depending on <m>\omega_0</m>.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Changing <m>\omega_0</m> makes the mass oscillate faster or slower,
            but it never stops oscillating. The crossings keep coming, forever.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Infinitely many times, regardless of <m>c_1</m>, <m>c_2</m>, or <m>\omega_0</m>.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Exactly. Since there's no damping, the amplitude stays fixed and
            the mass oscillates forever -- crossing <m>x = 0</m> twice per
            period, indefinitely. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Zero times. The mass never reaches exactly <m>x = 0</m> unless it starts there.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The mass absolutely does pass through <m>x = 0</m>! Watch the
            counter in the interactive. Unless the solution is identically zero
            (which requires <m>c_1 = c_2 = 0</m>), the oscillation crosses
            equilibrium repeatedly.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ALGEBRAIC JUSTIFICATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now let's justify this algebraically.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-equil-crossings-algebra">
    <title>Showing Infinitely Many Equilibrium Crossings</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m> be the
        general solution to an undamped mass-spring system, where
        <m>\omega_0 \gt 0</m> and <m>c_1, c_2</m> are not both zero.
        Show that <m>x(t) = 0</m> has infinitely many solutions.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Set <m>x(t) = 0</m> and solve for <m>t</m>. 
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We want to solve <m>x(t) = 0</m>, i.e.:
        <me>c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t) = 0.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Case 1: <m>c_2 = 0</m>.</alert>
        Then <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) = 0</m>. Since <m>c_1 \neq 0</m>
        (not both zero), we need <m>\cos(\omega_0 t) = 0</m>, which gives:
        <me>\omega_0 t = \frac{\pi}{2} + n\pi, \qquad n = 0, 1, 2, 3, \ldots</me>
        so <m>t = \dfrac{1}{\omega_0}\!\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2} + n\pi\right)</m>.
        This is a different value of <m>t</m> for each <m>n</m> -- infinitely many solutions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Case 2: <m>c_2 \neq 0</m>.</alert>
        Rearranging:
        <md>
          <mrow>c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) \amp= -c_2\sin(\omega_0 t).</mrow>
        </md>
        Dividing both sides by <m>c_2\cos(\omega_0 t)</m>:
        <me>\tan(\omega_0 t) = -\frac{c_1}{c_2}.</me>
       Solving for <m>t</m>:
<me>\omega_0 t = \arctan\!\left(-\frac{c_1}{c_2}\right) + n\pi</me>
<me>t = \frac{1}{\omega_0}\left[\arctan\!\left(-\frac{c_1}{c_2}\right) + n\pi\right].</me>
Since we need <m>t \geq 0</m>, we take <m>n</m> to be whichever non-negative
integers keep <m>t</m> positive -- starting at <m>n = 0</m> or <m>n = 1</m>
depending on the sign of the arctan term. Either way, there are infinitely
many valid values of <m>t</m>.
      </p>
      <solution>
        <title>Why <m>+ \, n\pi</m>? (if unsure: click for a trig review)</title>
        <p>
          Two things are going on here. First, <m>\arctan</m> only gives us
          one solution -- it's the inverse of <m>\tan</m> restricted to
          <m>(-\pi/2, \pi/2)</m>. So <m>\arctan(-c_1/c_2)</m> is just the
          <em>first</em> value of <m>\omega_0 t</m> that works.
        </p>
        <p>
          Second, tangent has period <m>\pi</m> -- meaning
          <m>\tan(\theta) = \tan(\theta + \pi)</m> for any <m>\theta</m>.
          So if <m>\omega_0 t = \theta_0</m> is a solution to
          <m>\tan(\omega_0 t) = -c_1/c_2</m>, then so is
          <m>\omega_0 t = \theta_0 + \pi</m>, and <m>\theta_0 + 2\pi</m>,
          and so on. That's where the <m>+ \, n\pi</m> comes from -- it
          captures all solutions at once.
        </p>
        <p>
          (Note: <m>\tan</m> has period <m>\pi</m>, not <m>2\pi</m> like
          sine and cosine. So we add multiples of <m>\pi</m>, not <m>2\pi</m>.)
        </p>
      </solution>
      <p>
        Since <m>n</m> ranges over all integers, this gives infinitely many
        values of <m>t</m> -- one for each <m>n</m>. No matter what
        <m>c_1</m>, <m>c_2</m>, and <m>\omega_0</m> are, the mass passes
        through equilibrium infinitely many times.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p><ul>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Graphical observation:</alert> free undamped simple harmonic
        motion oscillates with fixed amplitude forever. So the mass passes
        through equilibrium (<m>x = 0</m>) infinitely many times.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Algebraic justification:</alert> setting <m>x(t) = 0</m> and
        solving gives
        <me>t = \frac{1}{\omega_0}\left[\arctan\!\left(-\frac{c_1}{c_2}\right) + n\pi\right], \quad n = (0), 1, 2, \ldots</me>
        (or <m>t = \frac{1}{\omega_0}\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2} + n\pi\right)</m>
        when <m>c_2 = 0</m>). Either way, infinitely many solutions for the time in which it passes the equilibrium.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>This is a feature of free undamped motion:</alert> no energy
        is lost, so the oscillation never dies out. When we add damping later,
        this will change!
      </p></li>
    </ul></p>
  </conclusion>

